#{[17592186265853 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{{y}^{2}+1} & =e-y \\\\ x{e}^{{y}^{2}+1}+y & =e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=x{e}^{{y}^{2}+1}+y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=e.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,0)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{t}(1,0)={f}_{y}(1,0)\\mathbf{i}-{f}_{x}(1,0)\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf{t}(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{{y}^{2}+1}+y)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{{y}^{2}+1}+y)j \\\\  & =(2xy{e}^{{y}^{2}+1}+1)i-{e}^{{y}^{2}+1}\\,j \\\\ t(1,0) & =(2(1)(0){e}^{0+1}+1)i-{e}^{0+1}\\,j \\\\  & =i-e\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $\\mathbf{i}-e\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$"] [17592186265855 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y & =-8x-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}y+8x & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y+8x,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-4.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{t}(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf{t}(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{t}(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y+8x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y+8x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-(2xy+8)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $\\mathbf{j},$ we must have $\\mathbf{t}\\cdot \\mathbf{j}=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{t}(x,y)\\cdot \\mathbf{j} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-(2xy+8)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot \\mathbf{j} & =0 \\\\ -(2xy+8) & =0 \\\\ xy+4 & =0 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{4}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-\\frac{4}{x}$ into the equation of our curve gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y & =-8x-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}\\left(-\\frac{4}{x}\\right) & =-8x-4 \\\\ -4x & =-8x-4 \\\\ 4x & =-4 \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for $x=-1,$ we get $y=4,$ and the corresponding point is $(-1,4).$"] [17592186265867 "The directional derivative ${f}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{'}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{'}(a,b)=\\mathrm{∇}f(a,b)\\cdot \\mathbf{u}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{u}$ is a unit vector in the direction of $\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $\\mathbf{u}$ in the direction of $\\mathbf{v}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖\\mathbf{v}‖ & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{1}^{2}}=2 \\\\ \\mathbf{u} & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}\\hspace{-0.167em}f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ \\mathrm{∇}\\hspace{-0.167em}f(2,1) & =\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{'}(2,1) & =\\mathrm{∇}\\hspace{-0.167em}f(2,1)\\cdot \\mathbf{u} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1(\\sqrt{3})+2(1)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}+2}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186265868 "The directional derivative ${f}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{'}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{'}(a,b)=\\mathrm{∇}f(a,b)\\cdot \\mathbf{u}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{u}$ is a unit vector in the direction of $\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $\\mathbf{u}$ in the direction of $\\mathbf{v}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖\\mathbf{v}‖ & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}}=5 \\\\ \\mathbf{u} & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(0,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}\\hspace{-0.167em}f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}))\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}))\\text{ }\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ \\mathrm{∇}\\hspace{-0.167em}f(0,-1) & =\\frac{2(0)}{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{2(-1)}{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{'}(0,-1) & =\\mathrm{∇}\\hspace{-0.167em}f(0,-1)\\cdot \\mathbf{u} \\\\  & =-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}(3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2(4)}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186265869 "The directional derivative ${f}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{'}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{'}(a,b)=\\mathrm{∇}f(a,b)\\cdot \\mathbf{u}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{u}$ is a unit vector in the direction of $\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $\\mathbf{u}$ in the direction of $\\mathbf{v}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖\\mathbf{v}‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\mathbf{u} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}\\hspace{-0.167em}f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =(2x-y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(2y-x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ \\mathrm{∇}\\hspace{-0.167em}f(1,1) & =(2(1)-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(2(1)-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{'}(1,1) & =\\mathrm{∇}\\hspace{-0.167em}f(1,1)\\cdot \\mathbf{u} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1(1)+1(-1)}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186265871 "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(x,y)=(1,4)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,4).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(1,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{x}+2\\sqrt{y})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{x}+2\\sqrt{y})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,4) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1}}\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(1,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{∇}f(1,4)}{‖\\mathrm{∇}f(1,4)‖} & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})}{‖\\frac{1}{2}(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}})}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186265872 "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+3xy+2{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+3xy+2{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =(2x+3y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(3x+4y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ \\mathrm{∇}f(2,1) & =(2(2)+3(1))\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(3(2)+4(1))\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =7\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+10\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $7\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+10\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$"] [17592186265960 "If we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-3{y}^{2},$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=6.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(3,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(3,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(3,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\,i-6y\\,j \\\\ ∇f(3,1) & =2(3)\\,i-6(1)\\,j \\\\  & =6\\,i-6\\,j \\\\  & =⟨6,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(3,1)=⟨6,-6⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(3,1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-3}{6} & =\\frac{y-1}{(-6)} \\\\ -6(x-3) & =6(y-1) \\\\ -(x-3) & =y-1 \\\\ -x+3 & =y-1 \\\\ x+y-4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186265961 "First, we bring all variables to one side: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4xy+2{x}^{2} & =x+2y \\\\ 4xy+2{x}^{2}-x-2y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=4xy+2{x}^{2}-x-2y$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$   \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,-1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,-1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4xy+2{x}^{2}-x-2y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4xy+2{x}^{2}-x-2y)\\,j \\\\  & =(4y+4x-1)\\,i+(4x-2)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-1) & =(4(-1)+4(2)-1)\\,i+(4(2)-2)\\,j \\\\  & =3\\,i+6\\,j \\\\  & =⟨3,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Hence, $∇f(2,-1)=⟨3,6⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(2,-1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{3} & =\\frac{y-(-1)}{6} \\\\ 6(x-2) & =3(y+1) \\\\ 2(x-2) & =y+1 \\\\ 2x-4 & =y+1 \\\\ 2x-y-5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186265962 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =2y-x-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2y+x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2y+x$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-2.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(0,2)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,2).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2y+x)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2y+x)j \\\\  & =(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+1)i+(\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-2)j \\\\ ∇f(0,2) & =(\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}}+1)i+(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}}-2)j \\\\  & =i-j \\\\  & =⟨1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(0,2)=⟨1,-1⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(0,2).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-0}{1} & =\\frac{y-2}{-1} \\\\ x & =2-y \\\\ x+y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186265978 "The rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(-1,1)$ in the direction of $\\mathbf{v}$ is equal to the directional derivative ${g}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{'}(-1,1).$ \n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $\\mathbf{u}$ in the direction of $\\mathbf{v}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖\\mathbf{v}‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-\\sqrt{2})^{2}}=\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\mathbf{u} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨1,-\\sqrt{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{y}+\\frac{y}{x}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y}+\\frac{y}{x})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{y}-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\,\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}⟩ \\\\ ∇g(-1,1) & =⟨\\frac{1}{1}-\\frac{1}{(-1)^{2}},\\frac{1}{(-1)}-\\frac{(-1)}{{1}^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the directional derivative ${g}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{'}(-1,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{'}(-1,1) & =\\mathrm{∇}g(-1,1)\\cdot \\mathbf{u} \\\\  & =⟨0,0⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨1,-\\sqrt{2}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{0(1)+0(-\\sqrt{2})}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186266047 "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,4,1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,4,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,4,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{xy}+\\sqrt{yz})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{xy}+\\sqrt{yz}+)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\sqrt{xy}+\\sqrt{yz})\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{xy}}\\,i+(\\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{xy}}+\\frac{z}{2\\sqrt{yz}})j+\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{yz}}\\,k \\\\ ∇f(1,4,1) & =\\frac{4}{2\\sqrt{1(4)}}\\,i+(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1(4)}}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4(1)}})j+\\frac{4}{2\\sqrt{4(1)}}\\,k \\\\  & =i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{1}{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,4,1).$"] [17592186266050 "Let's set $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}x.$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=5.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(1,-1,2)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,-1,2).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,-1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}x)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}x)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}x)k \\\\  & =(2xy+{z}^{2})\\,i+({x}^{2}+2yz)\\,j+({y}^{2}+2zx)\\,k \\\\ ∇f(1,-1,2) & =(2(1)(-1)+(2)^{2})\\text{ }i+((1)^{2}+2(-1)(2))\\text{ }j+((-1)^{2}+2(2)(1))\\text{ }k \\\\  & =2\\,i-3\\,j+5\\,k \\\\  & =⟨2,-3,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(1,-1,2)$ and passes through the point $(1,-1,2)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =⟨1,-1,2⟩+t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{∇}f(1,-1,2)=⟨1,-1,2⟩+t\\hspace{0.167em}⟨2,-3,5⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"] [17592186266051 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y) & ={x}^{2}z+y-\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ {z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}z-y & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)={z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}z-y,$ our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}z-y)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}z-y)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}z-y)k \\\\  & =(2xy{z}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}y)-2xz)i+({x}^{2}{z}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}y)-1)j+(2z\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2})k \\\\ ∇f(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1) & =(2(-1)\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)(1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡((-1)^{2}\\frac{\\pi }{2})-2(-1)(1))i+((-1)^{2}(1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡((-1)^{2}\\frac{\\pi }{2})-1)j+(2(1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡((-1)^{2}\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(-1)^{2})k \\\\  & =(-\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+2)i+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-1)j+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-1)k \\\\  & =2\\,i-j+k \\\\  & =⟨2,-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)$ and passes through the point $(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =⟨-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩+t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{∇}f(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)=⟨-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩+t\\hspace{0.167em}⟨2,-1,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"] [17592186266052 "Let's set $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}yz.$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=-4.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(2,-1,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,-1,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}yz)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}yz)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}yz)k \\\\  & =2xyz\\text{ }i+{x}^{2}z\\text{ }j+{x}^{2}y\\,k \\\\ ∇f(2,-1,1) & =2(2)(-1)(1)\\text{ }i+(2)^{2}(1)\\text{ }j+(2)^{2}(-1)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =-4\\,i+4\\,j-4\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-4,4,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{(-4)}=\\frac{y-(-1)}{4}=\\frac{z-1}{(-4)} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{(-1)}=\\frac{y+1}{1}=\\frac{z-1}{(-1)} \\\\ 2-x=y+1=1-z\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186266053 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={y}^{2}-{z}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2}-{z}^{2}-x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)={y}^{2}-{z}^{2}-x$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(3,2,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(3,2,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(3,2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-{z}^{2}-x)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-{z}^{2}-x)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({y}^{2}-{z}^{2}-x)k \\\\  & =-i+2y\\,j-2z\\,k \\\\ ∇f(3,2,1) & =-i+2(2)\\,j-2(1)\\,k \\\\  & =-i+4\\,j-2\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,4,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}} \\\\ \\frac{x-3}{(-1)}=\\frac{y-2}{4}=\\frac{z-1}{(-2)} \\\\ \\frac{x-3}{1}=\\frac{y-2}{-4}=\\frac{z-1}{2} \\\\ x-3=\\frac{2-y}{4}=\\frac{z-1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186266054 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\right)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2xy}{{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}^{2}}-\\frac{2xy}{{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4xy}{{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186266055 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}(xy))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}+x \\\\  & =\\frac{1+{x}^{2}}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186266056 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+R\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}=\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy{z}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{y})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(y{z}^{3}) \\\\  & =2y{z}^{2}+x{e}^{y}+3y{z}^{2} \\\\  & =5y{z}^{2}+x{e}^{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186266057 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+R\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xz\\operatorname{cos}y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{x}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{z}^{2}{e}^{y}) \\\\  & =z\\operatorname{cos}y+{x}^{2}+2xz{e}^{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(1,0,-2),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F})(1,0,-2) & =(-2)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}0+{1}^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-2)\\cdot {e}^{0} \\\\  & =-2+1-4 \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186266058 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+R\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x(y{e}^{z}+\\operatorname{ln}y))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy{e}^{z})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(z(x+1)) \\\\  & =(y{e}^{z}+\\operatorname{ln}y)+x{e}^{z}+(x+1) \\\\  & =y{e}^{z}+\\operatorname{ln}y+x{e}^{z}+x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(1,1,0),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F})(1,1,0) & =1\\cdot {e}^{0}+\\operatorname{ln}1+1\\cdot {e}^{0}+1+1 \\\\  & =1+0+1+1+1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186266075 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+R\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the curl of $\\mathbf{F}$ is the vector field given by\n\n$$\ncurl\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}\\times \\mathbf{F}=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ x{e}^{yz} & yz & {e}^{yz}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{yz})-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(yz)]\\mathbf{i}-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{yz})-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{e}^{yz})]\\mathbf{j}+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(yz)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{yz})]\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(z{e}^{yz}-y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-(0-xy{e}^{yz})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(0-xz{e}^{yz})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(z{e}^{yz}-y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+xy{e}^{yz}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-xz{e}^{yz}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186266076 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $\\mathbf{F}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy))-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-2y \\\\  & =y(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186266078 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $\\mathbf{F}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+xy) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+y}-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the scalar curl at the point $(\\frac{1}{2},0),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})}_{(1/2,0)} & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2}+0)}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186266129 "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=\\mathbf{F}(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)+y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)+x\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)+y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)+xy+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)+x+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking  $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)+x$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)+x+{g}^{′}(y) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)+x \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ g(y) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)=C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)+xy+C.\n$$"] [17592186266130 "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=\\mathbf{F}(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={e}^{y}-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{y}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int ({e}^{y}-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{e}^{y}+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{y}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking  $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{y}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{y}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{g}^{′}(y) & =x{e}^{y}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ g(y) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)=C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x{e}^{y}+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C.\n$$"] [17592186266131 "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=\\mathbf{F}(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={e}^{y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{y}+2y\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{e}^{y}+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{y}+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking  $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{y}+2y$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{y}+{g}^{′}(y) & =x{e}^{y}+2y \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =2y \\\\ g(y) & ={y}^{2}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)={y}^{2}+C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x{e}^{y}+{y}^{2}+C.\n$$"] [17592186266132 "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{y}-{z}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-2yz\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+x+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{y}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{y}-{z}^{2}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{y}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{x}{y}-{z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =-{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int (-{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-y{z}^{2}+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=-y{z}^{2}+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+x-y{z}^{2}+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-2yz+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-2yz$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2yz & =-2yz+{h}^{′}(z) \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ h(z) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+x-y{z}^{2}+C.\n$$"] [17592186266133 "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x+y{e}^{z},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{z}+1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=xy{e}^{z}-2z\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2x+y{e}^{z})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+xy{e}^{z}+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{z}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{z}+1$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{z}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =x{e}^{z}+1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int \\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =y+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=y+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+xy{e}^{z}+y+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=xy{e}^{z}+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=xy{e}^{z}-2z$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xy{e}^{z}-2z & =xy{e}^{z}+{h}^{′}(z) \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =-2z \\\\ h(z) & =-{z}^{2}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xy{e}^{z}+{x}^{2}+y-{z}^{2}+C.\n$$"] [17592186268891 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(2,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(2,1) & ={e}^{1-1}+2(2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1-1) \\\\  & =1+4 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(2,1) & ={f}_{x}(2,1)(x-2) \\\\ z-4 & =5(x-2) \\\\ z & =5x-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(2,1,4)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1 \\\\ z &=5x-6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${\\mathbf{n}}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=5x-6$ (or $5x-z-6=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${\\mathbf{n}}_{2}=⟨5,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨5,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-i-5\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-5⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-⟨-1,0,-5⟩=⟨1,0,5⟩$ is also parallel to it."] [17592186268892 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=-2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(-2,2).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(-2,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-2,2) & =(-2)^{2}(2)+2(-2)-(2) \\\\  & =8-4-2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-2,2) & ={f}_{y}(-2,2)(y-2) \\\\ z-3 & =2(y-2) \\\\ z & =2y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,2,3)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-2 \\\\ z &=2y-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=-2,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${\\mathbf{n}}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=2y-1$ (or $2y-z-1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${\\mathbf{n}}_{2}=⟨0,2,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,2,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =j+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $\\mathbf{j}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.$"] [17592186268893 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(0,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,1) & ={0}^{2}+{1}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0+1) \\\\  & =0+1-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(0,1) & ={f}_{x}(0,1)(x-0) \\\\ z-7 & =1(x) \\\\ z & =x+7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(0,1,7)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1 \\\\ z &=x+7\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${\\mathbf{n}}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=x+7$ (or $x-z+7=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${\\mathbf{n}}_{2}=⟨1,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-i-\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-1⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-⟨-1,0,-1⟩=⟨1,0,1⟩$ is also parallel to it."] [17592186268894 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(1,0).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,0) & =1\\cdot {e}^{1\\cdot 0}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(1,0) & ={f}_{y}(1,0)(y-0) \\\\ z-(-1) & =(-1)y \\\\ z & =-y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,0,-1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1 \\\\ z &=-y-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${\\mathbf{n}}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=-y-1$ (or $y+z+1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${\\mathbf{n}}_{2}=⟨0,1,1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-j+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $-\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}.$"] [17592186269008 "We know that $\\mathbf{p}⊥\\mathbf{q}$ ($\\mathbf{p}$ and $\\mathbf{q}$ are perpendicular) if and only if $\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q}=0.$\n\nComputing the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y} \\\\  & =7\\cdot 4+n\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =28-4n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & =0 \\\\ 28-4n & =0 \\\\ 28 & =4n \\\\ n & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186269204 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(-3\\cdot 3-5\\cdot 4)\\mathbf{i}-(2\\cdot 3-5\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{j}+(2\\cdot 4-(-3)\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(-9-20)\\mathbf{i}-(6-(-5))\\mathbf{j}+(8-3)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-29\\mathbf{i}-11\\mathbf{j}+5\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186269253 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(2\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{i}-(4\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{j}+(4\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(-2+2)\\mathbf{i}-(-4+2)\\mathbf{j}+(8-4)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186271969 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 8 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 4 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 4 \\\\ 6 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}8 & -2 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(1-16)\\mathbf{i}-(-4-24)\\mathbf{j}+(32+12)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-15\\mathbf{i}+28\\mathbf{j}+44\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186272079 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 7 & 3 & -4 \\\\ -1 & -4 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}7 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 3-(-4)\\cdot (-4))\\mathbf{i}-(7\\cdot 3-(-4)\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{j}+(7\\cdot (-4)-(3)\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(9-16)\\mathbf{i}-(21-4)\\mathbf{j}+(-28+3)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-7\\mathbf{i}-17\\mathbf{j}-25\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186272141 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\